# Copy to /opt/opencode-remote-control/.env on the host (owner-only, chmod 600).
# The deploy workflow creates the file and generates RELAY_STATE_KEY on its
# first run, so a fresh host needs nothing here.

# Port the relay listens on. Changing it is a three-file change: the four
# proxy_pass lines in nginx/opencode.b4tr.net.conf and the published port in
# docker-compose.yml have to follow it, and the deploy's own health probe reads
# this file to find it (.github/workflows/deploy.yml).
# PORT=8080

# Encrypts the persisted session state (AES-256-GCM). Keep it OUT of the state
# volume: with it, join codes survive a redeploy and a copied volume is useless
# without the key. Generate with: openssl rand -hex 32
# RELAY_STATE_KEY=

# Where the session set is persisted inside the container (compose mounts a
# named volume there). Empty = in-memory only.
# RELAY_STATE_FILE=/data/state.json

# Which proxy hop to trust for X-Forwarded-For (express `trust proxy`). In
# Docker the nginx hop arrives from the bridge gateway (172.x), so the compose
# default is "loopback, uniquelocal". See DEPLOY.md.
# RELAY_TRUST_PROXY=loopback, uniquelocal

# Most shares the relay holds at once. A full relay makes room by ending the
# share whose bridge has been gone longest (over 5 minutes), and refuses new
# shares with 503 only when there is none; both are logged. See DEPLOY.md.
# RELAY_MAX_SESSIONS=2000

# DoS bounds on response buffering. The first caps the largest single frame a
# bridge may send (a larger frame is rejected before it is buffered, and it is
# also the ceiling a compressed body may inflate to). It defaults to the 100 MiB
# the bridge protocol allows: lower it only for an install whose own bridges are
# new enough to be told, because a frame over it costs that bridge its link, not
# just the request. The relay announces the value in its first frame; a bridge
# that understands it answers 413 instead of sending the frame, an older one
# still sends up to 100 MiB. The second caps the total response bytes the
# proxy path holds for slow/non-reading viewers at once: over it, a new request
# gets 503 rather than being buffered. It is never below eight of the first, so
# lowering the frame cap is what tightens it.
# RELAY_BRIDGE_MAX_PAYLOAD_BYTES=104857600
# Total response-body bytes the proxy path may hold for slow viewers at once.
# The shipped default is NOT this number: it is whichever is larger of it and
# eight frame caps, so 800 MiB with the default frame cap. Lower the frame cap
# to tighten it.
# RELAY_PROXY_MAX_BUFFERED_BYTES=134217728

# DoS bounds on the REQUEST half of the proxy path (the inbound twins above).
# The first caps ONE proxied body: a larger one is answered 413. The second caps
# the request bytes the whole process holds for in-flight proxied POSTs at once;
# the shipped default is NOT this number, it is whichever is larger of it and
# SIXTEEN body limits — 400 MiB with the body limit below — so lowering
# RELAY_PROXY_BODY_LIMIT_BYTES is what tightens it, and that is the only order in
# which the two stay coherent. One share may hold an eighth of that ceiling and
# never less than two whole bodies (50 MiB by default), and an eighth of it (at
# most 16 MiB) is held back for shares whose whole in-flight footprint is under
# 256 KiB, so a prompt or a permission answer still gets in while other shares
# fill the relay. The third caps how many proxied POSTs one share may have in
# flight, which bounds the small bodies the byte budgets barely notice.
# RELAY_PROXY_BODY_LIMIT_BYTES=26214400
# RELAY_PROXY_MAX_INBOUND_BYTES=134217728
# RELAY_PROXY_MAX_INFLIGHT_POSTS=32

# How often a proxied body is checked for progress, and how many checks without
# it are tolerated. Their product is the whole tolerance: 2.5 s x 4 = about ten
# seconds, which is deliberate (a phone on a cell handover goes quiet for
# seconds together). For a RESPONSE being read, progress is movement: a viewer
# reading slowly moves bytes at every check and is never cut, and only a
# response that moves no byte at all for the whole tolerance is. For a request
# BODY still arriving, progress is a RATE — about 4.3 KiB/s with the body limit
# above, a twentieth of the uplink this relay is sized for. A window under it
# leaves the body in arrears by the difference, later bytes pay that debt off,
# and a debt of the whole tolerance is answered 408 and cut. That floor is not a
# knob of its own: it is an eighth of RELAY_PROXY_BODY_LIMIT_BYTES per minute
# (at most 256 KiB), so it follows the body limit down.
#
# The relay also sets Node's server.requestTimeout itself rather than inheriting
# its 300 s: twice what a maximal body needs on that uplink, rounded to the
# minute — ten minutes with the body limit above, and derived from it the same
# way. It bounds ARRIVAL only, never a request waiting on its bridge or an event
# stream open for hours.
# RELAY_PROXY_STALL_CHECK_MS=2500
# RELAY_PROXY_STALL_STRIKES=4

# Keep-alive tuning for the two hops. The relay pings each bridge every
# PING_INTERVAL and terminates one that shows no sign of life for PONG_GRACE
# rounds; a GET caught by a drop waits RECONNECT_WAIT for the bridge to come
# back; a prompt waits PROMPT_TIMEOUT for its answer. Viewer event streams get a
# real `server.heartbeat` event every SSE_HEARTBEAT — NOT an SSE comment, which
# the web UI's reader parses as an event and dies on — so proxies do not close
# them, and advertise SSE_RETRY as the reconnect delay (the web UI doubles it,
# up to 30 s).
# RELAY_WS_PING_INTERVAL_MS=25000
# RELAY_WS_PONG_GRACE_ROUNDS=2
# RELAY_BRIDGE_RECONNECT_WAIT_MS=5000
# RELAY_PROMPT_TIMEOUT_MS=120000
# RELAY_SSE_HEARTBEAT_MS=15000
# RELAY_SSE_RETRY_MS=3000

# What one viewer's event stream may hold buffered (BUFFER), how much of one
# oversized frame that cap discounts while the viewer is still reading it
# (EXEMPT, clamped to the frame cap above), and what all streams together may
# hold over their caps (PARKED, never below what one whole frame puts over one
# stream's cap — under that floor, an event the bridge socket already accepted
# would drop every viewer it is written to). A stream over its own cap, or a
# frame that would take the total past PARKED, is dropped and the viewer
# reconnects; there is no replay of that event.
# RELAY_SSE_MAX_BUFFER_BYTES=2097152
# RELAY_SSE_MAX_EXEMPT_BYTES=33554432
# RELAY_SSE_MAX_PARKED_BYTES=134217728

# Send a Content-Security-Policy with the relay's OWN HTML shells (join page,
# session page, ended page), naming the sha256 of each script the relay injects.
# OFF by default: the same page loads the upstream opencode web UI, whose needs
# are not ours to guarantee, so turn it on only after checking in a browser that
# the UI still boots, renders a session and streams events. Proxied responses
# carry `default-src 'none'; sandbox` regardless of this flag. 1/true/yes/on and
# 0/false/no/off are the accepted values; anything else warns and keeps the
# default rather than being read as "off".
# RELAY_SHELL_CSP=0

# Artificial delay (ms) before rejecting a wrong access code. Spec default: 1000.
# Set to 0 only in tests.
# ACTIVATE_FAIL_DELAY_MS=1000

# Image to run. The deploy builds relay-local:amd64 on the host and tags the
# previous one relay-local:rollback-<stamp>; set this to roll back a MANUAL
# `docker compose up` on the host. It does not hold against a deploy: the
# workflow passes RELAY_IMAGE in the environment, which beats this file, so the
# next push to main puts the freshly built image back (see DEPLOY.md, Rollback).
# RELAY_IMAGE=relay-local:amd64

# NOTE: no API key is needed. Session registration is public (rate-limited per
# client IP); deleting a session requires its own bridge_token, issued at
# registration. RELAY_API_KEY is accepted for old bridges and ignored.
